# Project context for Cursor

## What this is

AU Small Business Grants Finder: a multi agent Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) system that helps Australian small businesses discover relevant grants and understand compliance requirements. Built and deployed on AWS. This is a real, live deployed portfolio project, not a throwaway demo.

## Problem it solves

Small business owners in Australia miss grants they qualify for because information is scattered across multiple government sources with inconsistent formats and no unified search.

## Data sources (see src/ingestion/sources)

- GrantConnect (grants.gov.au) — scraping, no public API (the US grants.gov has one, GrantConnect does not)
- data.gov.au — CKAN API (package_search)
- business.gov.au — scraping, no public API
- ATO small business guidance — scraping, no public API
- Fair Work Commission MAPD — scraping plus PDF parsing for award documents

## Architecture

Serverless first, chosen to fit a roughly $100 AUD budget.

- Lambda for lightweight processing and query handling
- ECS Fargate for the weekly ingestion job only (scraping/parsing needs more time and memory than Lambda comfortably gives)
- EventBridge Scheduler triggers the weekly ingestion run
- S3 for raw and processed data zones (raw/{source}/{date}/, processed/{source}/{date}/)
- DynamoDB for structured record lookups
- SQS to decouple ingestion completion from downstream processing
- SNS for pipeline failure alerts
- Bedrock for embeddings and generation
- Multi agent query routing at query time: eligibility agent, compliance agent, retrieval agent
- EKS used once only, for a same day teardown demonstration of container orchestration knowledge. Not part of the live running system.

Full data flow and the draft common record schema are in docs/architecture.md.

## Tech stack

Python for ingestion, processing, and the RAG pipeline. AWS for infrastructure. Infra as code (Terraform or AWS CDK) will live in /infra, not yet started.

## Current repo state

- README.md and docs/architecture.md are written and current
- src/ingestion/sources/base.py defines the shared ingester interface (BaseSourceIngester, RawRecord)
- grantconnect.py is implemented: scrapes the Go/List listing page and Go/Show detail pages using requests and BeautifulSoup. The selectors are a first pass and have not been verified against live HTML (GrantConnect was not reachable from the sandbox this was written in), so confirm them against the real site and adjust _extract_go_links and _extract_labeled_fields if needed
- The remaining four source ingesters (data_gov_au.py, business_gov_au.py, ato.py, fair_work_mapd.py) are stubs: class defined, correct interface, but fetch() raises NotImplementedError with a TODO describing what needs to be implemented
- src/ingestion/pipeline.py is a working orchestrator: it runs every source ingester, isolates failures per source so one bad source does not stop the others, and writes raw output to S3 as newline delimited JSON via src/ingestion/utils.py
- tests/test_pipeline.py verifies the failure isolation behavior and currently passes against the stubbed sources
- src/processing, src/rag, and src/lambda_handlers are empty package placeholders, not yet built
- infra/ has a README describing planned resources only, no actual IaC yet

## What to build next

Implement the fetch() method on each remaining source ingester, one at a time, starting with whichever is prioritized in conversation. data.gov.au has a real API and should be the most straightforward. GrantConnect, business.gov.au, ATO, and Fair Work MAPD are all scraping based; verify GrantConnect's selectors against the live site before trusting its output.

## Conventions to follow

- Match the existing style in base.py and the stub files: dataclasses for records, type hints, docstrings explaining what a module does and why
- Keep per source ingestion logic isolated in its own file under src/ingestion/sources, implementing BaseSourceIngester
- Do not let one source's failure break the pipeline run; pipeline.py already handles this, preserve that behavior when editing it
- Respect robots.txt and add reasonable delays for the scraping based sources
- This is a portfolio project meant to demonstrate the author's own work. Write code that is understandable and explainable line by line, not large unexplained blocks. Prefer small, real, incremental commits over one large dump, and write commit messages that describe the actual change

